{"name":"Back of Beyond","hex":"#015f7f","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"back of beyond","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very remote place."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/back_of_beyond"}],"entries":[{"title":"Back of Beyond (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Back of Beyond is a 1995 Australian film. According to Ozmovies, \"The film was released in Sydney and Melbourne on 2nd November 1995, but quickly disappeared.\" It was released on VHS in Australia and laserdisc in the United States but is now very difficult to find."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Back_of_Beyond_(film)"},{"title":"The Back of Beyond","blocks":[{"text":"The Back of Beyond (1954) is a feature-length award-winning Australian documentary film produced and directed by John Heyer for the Shell Film Unit. In terms of breadth of distribution, awards garnered, and critical response, it is Heyer's most successful film. It is also, arguably, Australia's most successful documentary: in 2006 it was included in a book titled 100 Greatest Films of Australian Cinema, with Bill Caske writing that it is \"perhaps our [Australia's] national cinema's most well known best kept secret\"."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c9/The_Back_of_Beyond_cover_low_res.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Back_of_Beyond"},{"title":"Human back","blocks":[{"text":"The human back, also called the dorsum, is the large posterior area of the human body, rising from the top of the buttocks to the back of the neck. It is the surface of the body opposite from the chest and the abdomen. The vertebral column runs the length of the back and creates a central area of recession. The breadth of the back is created by the shoulders at the top and the pelvis at the bottom."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Posterior_view_of_human_female_and_male_backs_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Posterior_view_of_human_female_and_male_backs_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_back"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Gray204.png/120px-Gray204.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Gray204.png/250px-Gray204.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Gray204.png/120px-Gray204.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray204.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Back_Muscles.jpg/120px-Back_Muscles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Back_Muscles.jpg/250px-Back_Muscles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Back_Muscles.jpg/120px-Back_Muscles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABack_Muscles.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Gray798-back_crop.png/120px-Gray798-back_crop.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Gray798-back_crop.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Gray798-back_crop.png/120px-Gray798-back_crop.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray798-back_crop.p